3. PyMuPDF is AGPL-3.0 — read this before deploying
---------------------------------------------------

PyMuPDF is dual-licensed: GNU Affero GPL v3.0, or a commercial license from
Artifex Software. This project uses the AGPL branch.

What AGPL section 13 means in practice: if you run a modified version of this
program as a network service that other people interact with, you must offer
those users the corresponding source code of your modified version. Running it
locally for yourself, or for a closed group inside one organization, does not
trigger that obligation.

If you plan to host this as a public Q&A service, you have three options:

  a) Publish your modifications under AGPL-3.0 as well. The simplest option,
     and the one this project assumes.
  b) Buy a commercial PyMuPDF license from Artifex and keep your changes closed.
  c) Replace PyMuPDF with a permissively licensed PDF text extractor. The
     project touches it in exactly two places — `insight_rag/parse/pdf_parser.py`
     and `insight_rag/index/transfer.py` — both call `page.get_text("blocks")`
     and split by x-coordinate. pdfplumber (MIT) or pypdf (BSD-3) can be
     substituted with modest effort.

This notice is a good-faith summary, not legal advice. If the distinction
matters commercially, ask a lawyer.


4. The corpus of academic papers
--------------------------------

The corpus is built from 14 published papers, listed in
`data/papers_manifest.json` (title, arXiv ID, page range). Building it requires
downloading the PDFs from arXiv yourself — `build_corpus.py` does that.

This repository redistributes the manifest and the derived index statistics,
never the papers themselves. The papers remain under their authors' and
publishers' copyright; arXiv's terms govern your own downloads. Derived
artifacts that this repository does commit — chunk counts, page counts,
evaluation numbers — are facts about the corpus, not reproductions of it.

The two sample documents in `data/demo/` are original fictional content written
for this project and carry the same MIT license as the code. They were generated
by `tools/make_demo_pdfs.py`, so you can regenerate or replace them freely.


